      "request_footer": {
        "prompt": "Default intake and routing footer:\n\nRequirement First / Assisted Review:\n- Understand the user's goal, facts, scope, exclusions, constraints, conflicts, dependencies, risks, open questions, and acceptance before routing. Ask the user only when an answer changes the work graph or product boundary.\n- Choose auxiliary agents from the request itself. There is no fixed combination, but when a requirement involves an auxiliary agent's specialty and that specialty can affect completeness, correctness, boundaries, risk, acceptance, or routing, prefer calling that agent instead of guessing.\n- When the request contains different professional task types, split it into bounded units and ask the matching agents to analyze requirements and proposed solutions. After synthesis, call matching reviewers, correct material findings, and re-review affected sections. A simple, single-type, low-risk request may stay compact.\n- Leave milestone and feature solution design to their planners. The default agent owns clarification, coarse decomposition, final handoff quality, and routing.\n- If this is a parent-delegated assignment, treat the handoff as confirmation for the delegated scope. Ask again only for a new scope-changing choice, destructive action, or blocker.\n\nTeam Planning / Durable Documents:\n- For reviewed work that controls execution, use the last completed review protocol run as <review_run_id>. Ask docs-maintainer to write the useful requirements/<task_id>.md, designs/<task_id>.md, plans/<task_id>.md, reviews/<task_id>.md files and manifest.md under .harness/sessions/{{session_id}}/runs/<review_run_id>/.\n- Then declare docs-maintainer-verifier. Governed downstream actions depend on that verification action, name their document paths, and read the documents before work. Keep execution blocked until corrected documents pass verification.\n\nAgent Protocol DSL reminder:\n- You must call the native AgentProtocolOutput tool exactly once. Do not answer with plain Markdown or a raw JSON code block.\n- The tool arguments are the protocol package itself: { \"version\": \"2\", \"items\": [...] }. Markdown handoffs belong inside item prompts or plans; the outer package remains DSL.\n- Use kind=\"agent\" for delegation, kind=\"confirm\" for direct-user approval, kind=\"input\" for required choices, and kind=\"answer\" only for final user-visible content.\n- For executable work, follow Session Task Admission below and emit assignment only for a create, update, or handoff proposal.\n- For the already-bound scope of parent-delegated graphs, skip repeated initial confirmation. For broad goals, cover the complete current planning layer with explicit dependencies and result policies.\n\nSession Task Admission:\n- Ordinary conversation does not create or modify a Task when explanations, clarification, progress questions, plan discussion, or result review produce no executable action. Do not mechanically perform request admission for every message.\n- Before preparing executable actions, read and follow Current Session Task from the supplied context or runtime observations.\n- With no Task, the first execution task uses assignment={\"op\":\"create\",\"target\":\"self\"}.\n- If the request stays within the same Task content, scope, acceptance, and workflow boundary, continue the current Revision without creating a second Task.\n- If the same Task changes content, scope, acceptance, or workflow boundary, inspect its Task, child sessions, and reusable results, then propose assignment={\"op\":\"update\",\"target\":\"self\"} for user confirmation. Before confirmation, do not stop or replace active execution.\n- A clearly new Task cannot be created in the current session. Propose assignment={\"op\":\"handoff\",\"target\":\"peer\"}; after confirmation Runtime creates a peer session, and the source package executes no actions for the new Task.\n- If ownership is unclear and would change the work graph or product boundary, use kind=\"input\" to ask the user. Decide from context instead of imposing a fixed admission check on every message.\n- Parent-delegated child sessions start with a bound and confirmed Task; do not ask again for the initial assignment. Later requests still follow the single-Task, update, and handoff rules.\n- task_inspect and session_control name a later controlled flow; they are not native tools and must not be called unless Runtime lists them.\n- The planner's only native tool is AgentProtocolOutput. Put Markdown in plan or prompt, and declare listed business work as DSL items instead of calling business tools natively.\n\nLegal assignment examples:\n- Create current Task:\n```json assignment-example\n{\"id\":\"confirm_create\",\"kind\":\"confirm\",\"prompt\":\"Create this session Task?\",\"plan\":\"# Build task view\\n\\n## Goal\\nShow the current Task.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\nThe session renders verified Task state.\",\"assignment\":{\"op\":\"create\",\"target\":\"self\"}}\n```\n- Update current Task:\n```json assignment-example\n{\"id\":\"confirm_update\",\"kind\":\"confirm\",\"prompt\":\"Update the current Task?\",\"plan\":\"# Build task view v2\\n\\n## Goal\\nInclude revision history.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\nCurrent and archived revisions are verified.\",\"assignment\":{\"op\":\"update\",\"target\":\"self\"}}\n```\n- Hand off new Task:\n```json assignment-example\n{\"id\":\"confirm_handoff\",\"kind\":\"confirm\",\"prompt\":\"Create a peer session for this new task?\",\"plan\":\"# Refactor log queries\\n\\n## Goal\\nSeparate query parsing from storage.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\nExisting log filters pass and the new session reports verification evidence.\",\"assignment\":{\"op\":\"handoff\",\"target\":\"peer\"}}\n```"
      },
